    hub_lecture_add_desc: "How to combine groups of objects",
    lecture_add_title: "Lesson: Addition",
    lecture_add_sub: "Learn to add numbers",
    add_s1_title: "Addition",
    add_s1_text: "Barsik knows the secret of addition! Addition is when we combine two groups of objects into one. Ready to find out how it works?",
    add_s2_title: "Combining Two Groups",
    add_s2_text: "If you have objects in one group and objects in another, put them together and count how many there are in total.",
    add_s3_title: "Example: Apples in Your Hands",
    add_s3_text: "One hand has 3 apples, the other has 2 apples. We add: 3 + 2 = 5. That's 5 apples in total!",
    add_s4_title: "The Plus Sign",
    add_s4_text: "The \"+\" sign means addition. When you see it between numbers, combine both groups and count the total.",
    add_s5_title: "Another Example",
    add_s5_text: "4 pencils in a case and 3 pencils on the table. 4 + 3 = 7. That's 7 pencils in total.",
    add_s6_title: "You Can Add!",
    add_s6_text: "Barsik is proud of you — now you know how to add numbers! Ready to test yourself in the game?",
    hub_compare_title: "Compare Numbers",
    hub_compare_desc: "Which number is bigger?",
    hub_lecture_compare_title: "Lesson: Comparing Numbers",
    hub_lecture_compare_desc: "Greater than, less than, equal signs",
    lecture_compare_title: "Lesson: Comparing Numbers",
    lecture_compare_sub: "Learn to compare numbers and pick the sign",
    compare_s1_title: "Comparison",
    compare_s1_text: "Barsik loves playing \"more or less\"! Today we'll learn to compare numbers and choose the right sign. Ready to start?",
    compare_s2_title: "Three Comparison Signs",
    compare_s2_text: "The \">\" sign means \"greater than,\" the \"<\" sign means \"less than,\" and the \"=\" sign means \"equal\" — the numbers are the same.",
    compare_s3_title: "Which Way the Sign Points",
    compare_s3_text: "A comparison sign always \"opens\" toward the bigger number and points its narrow tip at the smaller one. For example, 5 > 3.",
    compare_s4_title: "Example: Compare 4 and 7",
    compare_s4_text: "4 is less than 7, so we use the \"<\" sign: 4 < 7.",
    compare_s5_title: "Example: Compare 6 and 6",
    compare_s5_text: "The numbers are the same, so we put an \"=\" sign between them: 6 = 6.",
    compare_s6_title: "You Can Compare Numbers!",
    compare_s6_text: "Barsik is proud of you — now you know the greater-than, less-than, and equal signs! Ready to test yourself in the game?",
    addition_game_title: "Add and Find Out!",
